          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#pure_hodge_structure Pure Hodge structure
          %p
            A
            %em Hodge structure
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %em pure Hodge structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} grading
            structure on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            – called a
            %em Hodge decomposition
            – of the kind that is exhibited by the
            = succeed "/complex-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifolds"} Kähler manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theorem"} Hodge theorem
            %em
              of weight
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            if it behaves like the cohomology of a Kähler manifold of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          %p
            If instead of considering a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            one considers the cohomology groups of a parameterized collection of spaces – hence the cohomology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"}> sheaves
              \/
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
            – then one speaks of
            %em variation of Hodge structure
            (of a given weight).
          %p
            By a central theorem of Hodge theory (recalled as theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HodgeFiltrationForComplexSpaceReproducesKaehlerHodgeStructure"}
            below) the traditional (and original)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+complex"} filtration
            on the complex cohomology of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+differential+forms"} harmonic differential forms
            generalizes to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+complex"} filtration
            of the complex-valued
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+space"} complex analytic space
            which is simply given by the canonical degree-filtration of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+de+Rham+complex"} holomorphic de Rham complex
          %p
            This means that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            in the guise of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            is nothing but the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            of a stage of the Hodge filtration along the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            ” map from integral to complex cohomology. (A point of view highlighted for instance in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#PetersSteenbrink08"} Peters-Steenbrink 08, section 7.2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+characters"} Chern characters
            that appear in the general definition/characterization of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"} differential cohomology hexagon
            starting around the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "differential+cohomology+diagram#DeRhamCoefficients"} de Rham coefficients
          %p
            This modern point of view is also crucial for instance in the characterization of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intermediate+Jacobian"} intermediate Jacobian
            (see there) as the subgroup of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            that is in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of the map to Hodge-filtering stage of ordinary cohomology. See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "intermediate+Jacobian#CharacterizationAsHodgeTrivialDeligneCohomology"} intermediate Jacobian – characterization as Hodge-trivial Deligne cohomology
          %h3#mixed_hodge_structure Mixed Hodge structure
          %p
            A
            %em mixed Hodge structure
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtration"} filtration
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            – called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+filtration"} Hodge filtration
            – such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+graded+object"} associated graded object
            has pure Hodge structure of weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            in each degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+varieties"} complex varieties
            that have singularities (
            %a{:href => "#Deligne71"} Deligne 71
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Deligne74"} Deligne 74
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Historically, Hodge structures originate in the special structure induced on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            of a compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
            by the existence of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+differential+forms"} harmonic differential forms
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ForAKaehlerManifold"} on the cohomology of a Kähler manifold
          %p
            But it turns out that this Hodge structure only depends on the natural degree-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtration"} filtration
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+de+Rham+complex"} holomorphic de Rham complex
            and hence more generally there is canonical Hodge structure
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ForAComplexAnalyticSpace"}> on the cohomology of a complex analytic space
              \.
          %p Abstracting from here one defines Hodges structures
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OnAnAbelianGroup"}> generally on abelian groups
              \.
          %h3#ForAKaehlerManifold On the cohomology of a Kähler manifold
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
            and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{p,q}(X)
            for its space of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+differential+forms"} harmonic differential forms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+isomorphism"} Hodge isomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+cohomology"} Dolbeault cohomology
            in bidegree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
          %p
            Notice that by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
            there are canonical maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^{p,q}(X)\to H^{p+q}(X,\mathbb{C})
          %p
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with complex
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
          #TraditionalHodgeFiltration.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em Hodge filtration
              on the cohomology of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+complex"} filtered complex
              structure given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mi p
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mi q
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi p
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mi q
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
